#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180517} :activity/id #uuid "ae806ffe-8add-5f7b-a38d-5d4077cc72cb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050890 :topic/id #uuid "6eaa79dd-c90f-5e62-94cb-871b5a23b05e" :topic/math-academy-id 3729} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180733 :tutorial/content "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nHowever, if the domain of the variable is restricted, some expressions might allow further simplification. \n\nFor example, suppose we know that $a>0.$ Let's use this information to fully simplify $\\sqrt{36a^2}.$\n\nWe can start by simplifying the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{36{a}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(6a)^{2}} & = \\\\ |6a| & = \\\\ |6|\\cdot |a| \\\\ 6|a| & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $a>0,$ and we know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have $|a|=a.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n6|a| = 6a .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "414812ea-abcc-59df-98fa-8a79ae36a1a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5905 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c8a28231-be54-56e8-b63b-3e1a42d1360e" :step/math-academy-id 36113 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180548}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180521 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180736 :tutorial/content "Let's see what changes if we consider square roots of perfect squares when some variables are negative.\n\nFor example, suppose that $t \\lt 0.$ Let's use this to simplify the expression $\\sqrt{t^2}.$\n\nSimplifying this expression in the usual way, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sqrt{t^2}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\\sqrt{(t)^2}&=\\[5pt]\n\t|t|.&\n\t}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $t \\lt 0.$ To take the absolute value of a negative number, we need to flip the sign. This gives $|t| = -t.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{t^2}} = {\\color{red}-t}.\n$$\n\nYou can convince yourself that this is correct by plugging a few values of $t$ into the above equation. Provided that $t$ is negative, this expression will always give a true statement.\n\nFor example, suppose that $t = -2.$ Substituting into the left-hand side of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & = \\\\ 2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=-2$ into the right-hand side of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-t & = \\\\ -(-2) & = \\\\ 2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides give the same result, the statement is true." :tutorial/id #uuid "1f0207ef-9d6a-584b-8d44-a92c732cce4b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5928 :tutorial/title "The Square Root of a Perfect Square With Negative Variables"} :step/id #uuid "8e668d2a-9da1-5eaf-be19-c24fd0a3b12a" :step/math-academy-id 36179 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180547}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179761 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179461 :question/id #uuid "27032e03-f6b6-5f66-b514-d3b9c4f79ec0" :question/math-academy-id "e-1352"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7d2284f-c4ba-571f-91a6-be472f185c0b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501231}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Given That Some Variables Are Negative"} :step/id #uuid "9a309239-eefc-54be-8199-bbf772d5350e" :step/math-academy-id 4285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179762 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179462 :question/id #uuid "4b7c6339-6e9c-5047-a95f-3daf41db4a8a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1353"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b8f0a91-f0de-5907-9c96-eac8a3a0e7cf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049843}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501177}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Given That Some Variables Are Non-Negative"} :step/id #uuid "35d16b06-b444-5257-a405-5223a9834174" :step/math-academy-id 4287 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180521}}] :activity/title "The Square Root of a Perfect Square With Domain Restrictions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179879}]}
